              <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
                <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-xs">
                  <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm sm:text-base mb-1">1. Independence Palace (Reunification Palace)</h3>
                  <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                    The former presidential palace of South Vietnam, preserved as it stood on April 30, 1975 when tanks crashed through the gates. It provides a striking contrast to Cu Chi's guerrilla tactics. You walk through mid-century state reception rooms, the telecommunications war bunker in the basement, and vintage military hardware on the lawns. Takes about 75 to 90 minutes.
                  </p>
                </div>

                <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-xs">
                  <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm sm:text-base mb-1">2. War Remnants Museum</h3>
                  <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                    One of Vietnam's most visited museums, housing military aircraft, armored vehicles, weapon artifacts, and photojournalism exhibitions documenting the war's human cost. It deepens the historical context of what you saw in the Cu Chi forest. The photo galleries are graphic and emotionally heavy; families with younger children often prefer substituting this stop with cultural sights such as the historic <a href="/hcmc-museum-of-fine-arts-guide" class="text-amber-800 hover:text-amber-900 font-semibold underline underline-offset-2 decoration-amber-300">HCMC Museum of Fine Arts</a>.
                  </p>
                </div>

                <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-xs">
                  <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm sm:text-base mb-1">3. Saigon Central Post Office & French Quarter</h3>
                  <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                    Iconic 19th-century French colonial architecture with vaulted ceilings, painted historical maps, and active postal counters. It offers a relaxing architectural break after a morning of military history and takes just 30 to 45 minutes to enjoy. <em>(Note: The adjacent Notre-Dame Cathedral remains under scaffolding for its multi-year restoration, so visits are focused on the fully active Post Office and surrounding Paris Square).</em>
                  </p>
                </div>

                <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-xs">
                  <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 text-sm sm:text-base mb-1">4. Saigon River & Waterbus</h3>
                  <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">
                    Traveling along the Saigon River gives you views of modern city towers contrasting with traditional riverbank life. If you want to take the municipal Saigon Waterbus, check current timetables rather than expecting an impromptu sunset departure. Passengers must check schedules and buy tickets in advance. For a relaxed full-day tour, a chartered private speedboat or evening dinner cruise is much more reliable than the public commuter ferry.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

