          <!-- Section: Physical Effort -->
          <section id="physical-effort" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-5">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                Physical Demands: What to Expect On-Site
              </h2>
            </div>

            <div class="text-slate-700 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed space-y-4">
              <p>
                While a half-day trip involves fewer hours, you should keep the physical realities of the site in mind:
              </p>
              <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-600 text-sm">
                <li><strong>Terrain & Climate:</strong> Paths are hard-packed dirt and clay through shady secondary forest. Ground can be uneven with exposed tree roots. Midday visits can feel hot and humid, especially on open walking paths.</li>
                <li><strong>The Tunnel Crawl:</strong> Going underground is 100% optional. Some visitor tunnel sections have been widened for tourists, but they still feel very cramped. Travelers measuring 6 ft or wearing larger clothing sizes confirm that moving through the passages requires awkward duck-walking or crawling on hands and knees through warm, dimly lit underground air.</li>
                <li><strong>Surface Walking:</strong> Anyone uncomfortable with tight spaces or strenuous crawling can skip the underground tunnels entirely and enjoy the open-air exhibits, trapdoor demonstrations, bunker kitchens, and documentary film on easy surface paths.</li>
              </ul>

              {/* Who Should Skip The Crawl Box */}
              <div class="bg-rose-50 border border-rose-200 p-4 rounded-xl text-rose-950 text-xs sm:text-sm my-4 not-prose">
                <div class="font-bold flex items-center gap-2 text-rose-900 mb-1">
                  <span>⚠️</span> Who Should Skip The Crawl?
                </div>
                <p class="leading-relaxed">
                  The underground sections are optional. Taller or larger travelers may find the adapted tunnels surprisingly restrictive, while anyone uncomfortable with confined spaces can simply remain above ground.
