          <!-- Section: Ben Dinh vs Ben Duoc -->
          <section id="ben-dinh-vs-ben-duoc" class="scroll-mt-28 space-y-5">
            <div class="border-l-4 border-amber-500 pl-4 mb-4">
              <h2 class="font-serif text-2xl lg:text-[26px] font-bold text-slate-900 leading-tight">
                Ben Dinh vs. Ben Duoc: History, Not "Real vs. Fake"
              </h2>
            </div>

            <div class="text-slate-700 text-[15.5px] leading-relaxed space-y-4">
              <p>
                A common rumor online is that one tunnel site is "real" and the other is a "reconstruction."
              </p>
              <p>
                Neither is fake. The visitor tunnels at both Ben Dinh and Ben Duoc have been slightly widened and reinforced for tourism. According to UNESCO, both sectors are the two surviving areas that best retain the original scale and structure of the network.
              </p>
              <p>
                The real difference comes down to distance and history:
              </p>
              <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-slate-600 text-sm">
                <li><strong>Ben Dinh:</strong> Located closer to Ho Chi Minh City (~50 km northwest). It served as the base for the Cu Chi District Party Committee during the war. Because large tour buses can reach it easily, it draws the vast majority of day tours.</li>
                <li><strong>Ben Duoc:</strong> Located roughly 15 km farther out (~70 km total). It served as the headquarters for the regional military command. Because of the extra drive, it sees far fewer big tour coaches. It also houses the peaceful <strong>Ben Duoc Memorial Temple</strong> (built in 1993 to honor over 44,000 soldiers and locals who died in the area) and an expansive open-air heritage park.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>
                Ben Duoc is not just a quieter version of Ben Dinh; it offers a distinctly different historical perspective.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <!-- Section: Morning vs Afternoon -->
